== Chapter XIV

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #text(size: 0.9em)[The power of sin further demonstrated by the effects it has had in the lives of professors: first, in actual sins; secondly, in habitual declines.]
]

#smallcaps[We] must now go on to other evidences of the sad truth we are demonstrating. But since the main part of our work has been gone through, I will be briefer in handling the arguments that remain.

The next thing we may fix on, then, is the demonstration this law of sin has given in all ages of its power and efficacy, by the woeful fruits it has brought forth even in believers themselves. These are of two sorts. First, the great #emph[actual outbreaks] of sin in their lives. Secondly, their #emph[habitual declines] from the frames, state, and condition of obedience and communion with God that they had attained. By the rule of #emph[James] opened up before, both are to be laid to the account of this law of sin, and belong to the fourth head of its progress. Both are convincing evidences of its power and efficacy.

#emph[First,] consider the fearful #emph[outbreaks] of actual sin there have been in the lives of believers, and we will find our position proved. If I were to go through this consideration at length, I would have to recount all the sad and scandalous failings of the saints left on record in Holy Scripture. But their particulars are known to all, so I will not need to mention them, nor the many aggravations that go with them in their circumstances. Only a few things may be noted that will help make our present consideration of them useful. For example:

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, most of them are in the lives of men who were not of the #emph[lowest rank] or ordinary sort of believers. They were men who had a special #emph[eminence] on account of their walking with God in their generation. Such were #emph[Noah, Lot, David, Hezekiah,] and others. They were not men of ordinary size, but taller than their brothers from the shoulders up in profession, and indeed in real holiness. And surely that must be of mighty efficacy which could hurry such #emph[giants] in the ways of God into such abominable sins as they fell into. No ordinary engine could ever have turned them out of the course of their obedience. It was a poison that no #emph[athletic] constitution of spiritual health and no #emph[antidote] could withstand.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, these very men did not fall into their great sins at the #emph[beginning of their profession.] They did not fall when they had had only a little experience of the goodness of God, of the sweetness and pleasantness of obedience, and of the power and craft of sin, its drivings, solicitations, and surprises. They fell after a long course of walking with God and of acquaintance with all these things, together with countless motives to #emph[watchfulness. Noah,] by the length of men’s lives in those days of the world, had walked uprightly with God some #emph[hundreds of years] before he was surprised as he was (Gen 9). Righteous #emph[Lot] seems to have been toward the end of his days before he defiled himself with the abominations recorded. #emph[David,] in a short life, had as much experience of grace and of sin, and as much close spiritual communion with God, as any of the sons of men ever had, before he was thrown to the ground by this law of sin. So it was with #emph[Hezekiah] in his degree, which was by no means a low one. Now to set upon such persons, so well acquainted with its power and deceit, so armed and provided against it, who had been conquerors over it for so many years, and to prevail against them, shows a power and efficacy too #emph[mighty] for anything to withstand but the Spirit of the #emph[Almighty.] Who can expect to have a greater stock of #emph[inherent grace] than those men had? Who can expect more experience of God and of the excellence of his ways, of the sweetness of his love, and of communion with him, than they had? Who has either better equipment to oppose sin with, or more obligation to do so, than they? And yet we see how fearfully they were prevailed against.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Thirdly, as if God had permitted their falls on purpose, so that we might learn to be wary of this powerful enemy, they all fell when they had #emph[newly received great and astonishing mercies] from the hand of God. These ought to have been strong obligations to diligence and watchfulness in close obedience. #emph[Noah] had only just come out of that #emph[world of waters] in which he saw the ungodly world perishing for their sins, and himself preserved by that astonishing miracle which all ages must wonder at. While the world’s desolation was an hourly reminder to him of his strange preservation by the immediate care and hand of God, he falls into #emph[drunkenness. Lot] had just seen what no one can think of without trembling. He saw, as one puts it, “hell coming out of heaven” upon unclean sinners: the greatest evidence God ever gave in his providence of the judgment to come, except the cross of Christ. He saw himself and his children delivered by the special care and miraculous hand of God. And yet while these strange mercies were fresh upon him, he fell into #emph[drunkenness and incest. David] was delivered from all his troubles and had the necks of his enemies all around given to him; and he uses his peace from a world of trials and troubles to contrive #emph[murder and adultery.] It was immediately after #emph[Hezekiah’s] great and miraculous deliverance that he falls into his #emph[carnal pride and boasting.] Their falls at such seasons, I say, seem to have been permitted on purpose, to instruct us all in the truth we have in hand. No persons, at any season, with whatever equipment of grace, can promise themselves security from its prevailing power, except by keeping close constantly to him who has supplies to give out that are beyond its reach and efficacy. I think this should make us look about us. Are we better than #emph[Noah,] who had this testimony from God, that he was “blameless in his generation, and walked with God?” Are we better than #emph[Lot,] whose righteous soul was tormented by the evil deeds of ungodly men, and who is commended for it by the Holy Spirit? Are we more holy, wise, and watchful than #emph[David,] who obtained this testimony, that he was “a man after My own heart?” Or better than #emph[Hezekiah,] who appealed to God himself that he had served him uprightly with a perfect heart? And yet we see what prevailing power this law of sin worked in them and over them. There is no end of similar examples. They are all set up as #emph[buoys] to show us the sands, the shoals, and the rocks on which they suffered their shipwreck. It was to their hazard, danger, and loss; indeed, it would have been to their ruin, if God had not been pleased in his faithfulness graciously to prevent it. And this is the first part of this evidence of the power of sin, from its effects.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, sin shows its #emph[power] in the #emph[habitual declines] found in many believers: declines from zeal and holiness, from the frames, state, and condition of obedience and communion with God they had attained. #emph[Promises of growth] and progress are many and precious; the #emph[means] are excellent and effectual; the #emph[benefits] are great and unspeakable. Yet it often happens that, instead of this, decays and declines are found in professors, and indeed in many of the saints of God. Now this must come principally and chiefly from the strength and efficacy of indwelling sin, and is therefore a great evidence of it. So I will first prove the observation itself to be true: that some of the #emph[saints themselves] often decline from the growth and progress in faith, grace, and holiness that might rightly be expected of them. Then I will show that the cause of this evil lies in what we are treating of. That indwelling sin is the cause of #emph[total apostasy] in unsound professors will be declared afterward. But the work we have in hand is a greater one. To prevail on #emph[true believers] to a sinful decline and a #emph[gradual apostasy] requires more strength and efficacy to be put forth than to prevail on #emph[unsound professors] to #emph[total apostasy.] The wind that will blow a #emph[dead tree] with no root to the ground will hardly shake or bend a living, #emph[well-rooted tree.] But this it will do. Scripture mentions the first ways of #emph[David,] and commends them above #emph[his later ones] (2 Chr 17:3). The #emph[last ways] even of #emph[David] were tainted by the power of #emph[indwelling sin.] Although we are told only of the actual outbreak of sin, yet the uncleanness, and the #emph[pride] that was working in him when he numbered the people, were certainly rooted in a #emph[decline] from his first frame. Those rushes did not grow without mire. #emph[David] would not have done so in his #emph[younger days,] when he followed God in the wilderness of temptations and trials, full of faith, love, humility, brokenness of heart, zeal, and tender affection for all the ordinances of God, all of which were eminent in him. But his strength is impaired by the efficacy and deceitfulness of sin; his #emph[locks] are cut, and he becomes a prey to vile lusts and temptations. We have a notable instance in most of the churches that our Savior rouses to consider their condition in the Revelation. We may single out one of them. There were many good things in the church of #emph[Ephesus] (ch. 2:2, 3), for which it is greatly commended. Yet it is charged with a #emph[decay,] a #emph[decline,] a gradual falling off and apostasy (vv. 4, 5): “You have abandoned your first love. Therefore, keep in mind how far you have fallen. Repent and perform the deeds you did at first.” By the testimony of Christ himself there was a decay, both #emph[inward,] in the frame of heart as to faith and love, and #emph[outward,] as to obedience and works, compared with what they had before. The same might be shown about the rest of those #emph[churches,] except only one or two of them. #emph[Five] of them are charged with decays and declines. That is why Scripture mentions with great commendation the “devotion of your youth” and “your love as a bride” (Jer 2:2, 3); our “first faith” (1 Tim 5:12); the “assurance we had at first” (Heb 3:14). And warnings are given that we “do not lose what we have worked for” (2 John 8). But why need we look back or search for instances to confirm the truth of this observation? An #emph[habitual decline] from first engagements to God, from first attainments of communion with God, from first strictness in the duties of obedience, is ordinary and common among professors. Suppose we took a general view, for this purpose, of the professors in these nations, among whom the lot of the best of us will be found to fall, in part or in whole, in something or in everything. We might be abundantly convinced of the truth of this observation. Is their #emph[zeal] for God as warm, living, vigorous, effectual, and earnest as it was when they first gave themselves to God? Or rather, has not a common, careless, selfish frame of spirit come upon most professors in its place? Iniquity has abounded, and their love has grown cold. Was it not once a burden to their spirits to hear the name and ways and worship of God blasphemed and profaned? Could they not have said with the psalmist (Ps 119:136), “My eyes shed streams of tears because Your law is not obeyed”? Were their souls not anxious about the cause of Christ in the world, like #emph[Eli’s] about the ark? Did they not contend earnestly for the “truth once delivered to the saints,” and every part of it, especially where the grace of God and the glory of the gospel were especially concerned? Did they not labor to judge and condemn the world by a #emph[holy] and #emph[separate] way of life? And does the general run of professors now remain in this frame? Have they grown in it and made progress? Or has not a coldness and indifference about this grown upon the spirits of many? Indeed, do not many despise all these things, and look on their own former zeal as folly? May we not see many who were once esteemed in the ways of profession becoming daily a scorn and reproach to the men of the world through their misconduct, and that justly? Is it not with them as it was of old with the daughters of #emph[Zion] (Isa 3:24), when God judged them for their sins and wantonness? Have not the world and #emph[self] utterly ruined their profession? And do they not disregard the things in which they once declared a singular concern? Indeed, have not #emph[some] come, partly on one pretense and partly on another, to an open #emph[enmity] toward and hatred of the ways of God? These ways please them no more, but are evil in their eyes. But not to mention such #emph[open apostates] any further, whose hypocrisy the Lord Jesus Christ will shortly judge, how is it with the best? Have not almost all men grown cold and slack in these things? Are they not less concerned in them than before? Have they not grown weary and selfish in their religion, so that, as long as things are #emph[fairly well] at home, they hardly care how they go abroad in the world? At least, do they not prefer their ease, reputation, safety, and worldly advantages to these things? Christ abhors that frame, and declares that those in whom it prevails are not his. Some, indeed, seem to keep a good zeal for truth. But where they make the fairest appearance, there they will be found most abominable. They cry out against #emph[errors,] not for truth’s sake, but for the sake of parties and interests. Let a man be of their #emph[party] and promote their interest, and however #emph[corrupt] he is in his judgment, he is embraced, and perhaps admired. This is not #emph[zeal for God,] but for a man’s self. It is not “zeal for Your house has consumed me,” but “Master, … we tried to stop him, because he does not accompany us.” It would no doubt be better for men never to claim any #emph[zeal] at all than to put such wrathful selfishness in its place.]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, is men’s #emph[delight] in the ordinances and worship of God the same as in former days? Do they find the same sweetness and #emph[relish] in them as they did of old? How #emph[precious] the #emph[word] was to them once! What joy and delight they had in attending on it! How they would have run and gone to share in it, wherever it was dispensed in its power and purity, in the #emph[evidence and demonstration] of the Spirit! Did they not call the #emph[Sabbath] their delight, and was not its approach a real joy to their souls? Did they not long for the fellowship and communion of the saints? And could they not undergo many dangers to obtain it? And does this frame still remain on them? Are there not decays and declines to be found among them? May it not be said, “Their hair is streaked with gray, but they do not know it?” Indeed, are not men ready to say with those of old, “What a weariness it is!” (Mal 1:3). “It is a #emph[burden and a weariness] to be tied to observing all these ordinances. Why must we be so very strict in observing the #emph[Sabbath?] Why must we hear so often? Why this distinction in hearing?” Without their noticing it, a great disrespect, and even a contempt, of the pleasant and excellent ways of Christ and his gospel has fallen on many professors.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Thirdly, may not the same conviction be carried further by an inquiry into the #emph[whole course of obedience,] and into the performance of the duties men have been engaged in? Does the same #emph[conscientious tenderness about sinning] remain in many as in days of old? The same exact performance of #emph[private duties?] The same love for the brothers? The same readiness for the cross? The same humility of mind and spirit? The same self-denial? The steam of men’s lusts, with which the air is tainted, will not allow us to say so.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[We need go no further, then, than this wretched generation in which we live to prove the truth of the observation laid down as the foundation of the instance we are dwelling on. The Lord give repentance before it is too late!]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Now all these #emph[declines,] all these decays found in some professors, proceed from this root and cause. They are all the product of #emph[indwelling sin,] and all prove its exceeding power and efficacy. To prove this I will not need to go further than the #emph[general rule] we have already considered from #emph[James]: that #emph[lust,] or indwelling sin, is the cause of all #emph[actual] sin and all habitual declines in believers. This is what the apostle means to teach and declare in that place. So I will handle these two things. First, I will show that this proves a great #emph[efficacy and power] in sin. Secondly, I will declare the ways and means by which it #emph[brings forth,] or brings about, this cursed effect. All of it serves our general aim: to call on and warn believers to avoid it and to oppose it.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, it appears to be a work of great #emph[power and efficacy] from the provision that is made against it, over which it prevails. In the covenant of grace plentiful provision is made, not only to prevent #emph[declines] and decays in believers, but also to carry them on continually toward perfection. For example:]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, the #emph[word] itself, and all the ordinances of the gospel, are appointed and given to us for this end (Eph 4:11, 12, 13, 14, 15). The end for which #emph[gospel officers] are given to the church is also the end for which all the #emph[ordinances] they administer are given. For they are given “for works of ministry,” that is, for administering the ordinances of the gospel. Now what are these ends? They are all for preventing decays and #emph[declines] in the saints, and all for carrying them on to perfection, as is said in v. 12. In general it is “for the perfecting of the saints,” carrying on the work of grace in them and the work of holiness and obedience by them; or it is “to build up the body of Christ,” building them up in an increase of faith and love, every true member of the mystical body. But how far are the word and ordinances appointed to carry them on and to build them up? Do they have fixed bounds to their work? Do they carry them so far and then leave them? No, says the apostle (#emph[verse] 13). The dispensation of the word of the gospel and its ordinances is designed for our help, assistance, and furtherance until the whole work of faith and obedience is completed. It is appointed to #emph[perfect] and #emph[complete] the faith, knowledge, and growth in grace and holiness allotted to us in this world. But what if opposition and temptations lie in the way, with #emph[Satan] and his instruments working with great subtlety and deceit? Why, #emph[verse] 14: these #emph[ordinances] are designed to safeguard and deliver us from all their attempts and assaults. So, being preserved in the use of them, “speaking the truth in love, we will in all things grow up into Christ Himself, who is the head.” This, in general, is the use of all #emph[gospel ordinances,] the chief and main end for which God #emph[gave] and appointed them: to preserve believers from all #emph[decays] of faith and obedience, and to carry them on still toward #emph[perfection.] These are means the #emph[good vinedresser,] God, uses to make the #emph[vine] thrive and bear fruit. I could also show that this is the special end of each of them separately. Briefly, the #emph[word] is #emph[milk] and #emph[solid food] for nourishing and strengthening believers of all sorts and all degrees. It has both #emph[seed] and water in it, and fertilizing with it, to make them fruitful. The ordinance of the #emph[Supper] is appointed for the very purpose of #emph[strengthening] our faith in remembering the death of the Lord, and for exercising love toward one another. The #emph[communion of saints] is for building one another up in faith, love, and obedience.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, there is something that adds weight to this consideration. God does not allow us to be #emph[unmindful] of this help he has given us, but is continually #emph[calling on us] to use the means appointed for reaching the end proposed. He shows them to us, as the #emph[angel] showed the spring of water to #emph[Hagar.] Commands, exhortations, promises, and threats are multiplied for this purpose; see them summed up in Heb 2:1. He is continually saying to us, “Why will you#super[+] die? Why will you#super[+] #emph[wither and decay?] Come to the #emph[pastures] provided for you#super[+], and your souls will live.” If we see a #emph[lamb] run from the fold into the wilderness, we do not wonder if it is torn apart by wild beasts. If we see a #emph[sheep] leaving its #emph[green pastures] and streams of water to stay on dry, barren #emph[heaths,] we think it no wonder, and inquire no further, if we see it lean and ready to perish. But if we find #emph[lambs] wounded in the #emph[fold,] we wonder at the boldness and rage of the #emph[beasts of prey] that dared to attack them there. If we see #emph[sheep] wasting away in #emph[full pastures,] we judge them to be diseased and unsound. It is indeed no wonder that poor creatures who forsake their #emph[own mercies,] and run away from the pasture and fold of Christ in his ordinances, are torn apart by various #emph[lusts] and waste away with hunger and famine. But to see men living under and enjoying all the means of spiritual #emph[thriving,] and yet #emph[decaying,] not fat and flourishing but rather wasting and withering daily: this shows some secret, powerful disease whose poisonous and harmful qualities hinder the virtue and efficacy of the means they enjoy. This is #emph[indwelling sin.] It is so wonderfully powerful, so effectually #emph[poisonous,] that it can bring #emph[leanness] on the souls of men in the middle of all the precious means of growth and flourishing. It may well make us tremble to see men living under the means of the gospel and using them, preaching, praying, the administration of the sacraments, and yet growing colder in zeal for God every day, more selfish and worldly, and even #emph[habitually declining] from the degrees of holiness they had attained.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Thirdly, together with the dispensation of the #emph[outward means] of spiritual growth or progress, supplies of grace are also continually given to the #emph[saints] from their Head, Christ Jesus. He is the Head of all the saints. And he is a #emph[living Head,] so living that he tells us that “because he lives, we also will live” (John 14:19). He communicates #emph[spiritual life] to all who are his. In him is the fountain of our life, which is therefore said to be “hidden with Christ in God” (Col 3:3). And he gives this life to his saints by #emph[making them alive] by his Spirit (Rom 8:11). And he continues it to them by the supplies of #emph[living grace] that he communicates to them. From these #emph[two,] his #emph[making us alive] and his continually giving out supplies #emph[of life to us,] he is said to live in us (Gal 2:20): “I no longer live, but Christ lives in me.” The #emph[spiritual life] I have is not my own. It was not drawn out of myself, nor is it maintained by myself, but it is purely and solely the work of Christ. So it is not “I who live, but he lives in me,” the whole of my life being from him alone. Nor does this #emph[living Head] communicate only a #emph[bare life] to believers, so that they should merely #emph[live] and no more, a poor, weak, dying life, as it were. He gives out enough to afford them a strong, vigorous, thriving, flourishing life (John 10:10). He comes not only that his sheep may have life, but that “they may have it in all its fullness”\; that is, in a plentiful way, so that they may flourish and be fat and fruitful. So it is with the whole body of Christ and every member of it (Eph 4:15, 16): “we will in all things grow up into Christ Himself, who is the head. From Him the whole body, fitted and held together by what every joint supplies, according to the effectual working of each part, makes the body grow and builds itself up in love.” The end of all communications of grace and supplies of life from this living and blessed Head is the increase of the whole body and of every member of it, and its building itself up in love. His treasures of grace are unsearchable; his stores are inexhaustible. His #emph[life,] the fountain of ours, is full and eternal; his #emph[heart] is bountiful and large; his #emph[hand] is open and generous. So there is no doubt that he communicates supplies of grace #emph[abundantly] to all his saints for their increase in holiness. How is it, then, that they do not all flourish and #emph[thrive] accordingly? It is here as you may often see it in a natural body. Though the seat and source of the blood and spirits in the #emph[head] and #emph[heart] are excellently good and sound, there may yet be a #emph[withering limb] in the body; something intercepts the influences of life to it. So although the #emph[heart] and #emph[head] perform their office in giving #emph[supplies] no less to that limb than to any other, all the effect produced is merely to keep it from #emph[perishing utterly]\; it grows weak and decays every day. The withering and decaying of any member of Christ’s #emph[mystical body] is not for lack of his communicating grace for an #emph[abundant life.] It is from the powerful #emph[interception] of its efficacy by the interposing and opposition of #emph[indwelling sin.] That is why, where #emph[lust] grows strong, a great deal of grace will only keep the soul alive, and will not give it any eminence in fruitfulness at all. Christ often gives very #emph[much grace] where not #emph[many] of its effects appear. Grace spends its strength and power in withstanding the continual assaults of violent corruptions and lusts, so that it cannot put forth its proper virtue toward further fruitfulness. Think of a #emph[powerful medicine,] fit both to check harmful and infectious humors and to comfort, refresh, and strengthen nature. If the evil humor is strong and greatly prevailing, the medicine spends its whole strength and virtue in subduing and correcting it, and contributes much less to the relief of nature than it would if it did not meet such opposition. So it is with #emph[the eye salve] and the healing grace we have #emph[abundantly] from the #emph[wings of the Sun of Righteousness.] It is often forced to put forth its #emph[virtue] to oppose and contend against prevailing lusts and corruptions, and to subdue them in any measure. That is why the soul does not receive the #emph[strengthening] for duties and fruitfulness that it might otherwise receive from it. How sound, healthy, and flourishing, how fruitful and exemplary in holiness, many a soul might be by and with the grace continually communicated to it from Christ! Yet now, because of the power of indwelling sin, that soul is only #emph[not dead,] but weak, withering, and useless. And this, if anything, is a notable evidence of the efficacy of #emph[indwelling sin]: that it is #emph[able] to put such a stop and check on the mighty and effectual power of grace. Despite the blessed and continual supplies we receive from our Head, many believers #emph[decline] and decay, and that #emph[habitually,] from what they had attained; their #emph[last ways] do not match their #emph[first.] This is what makes the vineyard on the very #emph[fertile hill] bring forth so many wild grapes. This is what makes so many trees barren in fertile fields.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Fourthly, there are the #emph[continual supplies of grace] that are constantly communicated to believers according to the terms of the #emph[covenant,] which keep them from ever thirsting again in #emph[total want.] But besides these, there is also #emph[a readiness] in the Lord Christ to give special help to the souls of his own, as their occasions require. The apostle tells us that he is “a merciful High Priest” and “able” (that is, #emph[ready,] prepared and willing) “to help those who are being tempted” (Heb 2:18). On that account we are invited to come “with confidence” to the throne of grace, “so that we may receive mercy and find grace to help us in our time of need”\; that is, #emph[grace] that is sufficient, timely, and fitted to any special trial or temptation we may be exercised with. Our merciful High Priest is ready to give out this special, timely grace, over and above the constant communications of supplies of the Spirit we mentioned before. Besides the #emph[never-failing springs] of ordinary covenant grace, he also has special refreshing showers for times of drought. This is exceedingly to the advantage of the saints, for their preservation and growth in grace; and very many more things of the same nature might be added. But now, I say, in spite of all these, and the rest of similar importance, such is the power and efficacy of #emph[indwelling sin,] so great is its #emph[deceitfulness] and restlessness, so many are its wiles and #emph[temptations,] that it often happens that many of those for whose growth and progress all this #emph[provision] is made still, as was shown, go back and #emph[decline,] even in their course of walking with God. #emph[Samson’s] strength proved itself fully when he broke the #emph[seven fresh bowstrings] and the #emph[new ropes] he was bound with, as if they were “burning tow and thread.” A harmful humor in the body so stubborn that no use of the most effective remedies can prevail against it ought to be taken seriously. Such is this #emph[indwelling sin,] if it is not watched over. It breaks all the cords made to bind it; it blunts the instruments appointed to root it up; it resists all healing medicines, however effective; and it is therefore assuredly of exceeding efficacy. Besides, believers have #emph[countless obligations] on them, from the love and the command of God, to #emph[grow in grace] and to press on toward perfection, just as they have #emph[abundant means] provided for them to do so. Doing so is a matter of the greatest advantage, profit, sweetness, and contentment to them in the world. It is the burden and trouble of their souls that they do not do so, that they are not more holy, more zealous, more useful and fruitful; they desire it above life itself. They know it is their duty #emph[to watch] against this enemy, to fight against it, and to pray against it, and they do so. They desire its #emph[destruction] more than the enjoyment of all this world and all it can offer. And yet, in spite of all this, such is the subtlety and fraud, the violence and fury, the urgency and importunity of this adversary, that it often prevails to bring them into the woeful condition mentioned. So it is sometimes with believers as it is with men in some places at sea. They have a good, fair #emph[wind,] perhaps all night long. They work their #emph[rigging] and attend diligently to their business, and perhaps take great satisfaction in considering how they are getting on in their voyage. In the morning, or after a time, they come to measure what headway they have made and what progress they have had. They find that, instead of getting one step #emph[forward,] they are much #emph[farther back] than they were. They have fallen into a swift tide or current against them, which has #emph[frustrated] all their labors and made the wind in their sails almost useless. By it they have held up somewhat against the stream, but they have made no progress. So it is with believers. They have a good wind of #emph[supplies] of the Spirit from above. They attend to #emph[duties] diligently, pray constantly, hear attentively, and leave out nothing that may carry them on their #emph[voyage] toward eternity. But after a while, when they come to consider seriously, by examining their hearts and ways, what #emph[progress] they have made, they find that all their help and duties have not been able to hold them up against some #emph[strong tide] or current of #emph[indwelling sin.] It is true that these have kept them from being driven and wrecked on #emph[rocks] and #emph[shoals]\; they have preserved them from gross, scandalous sins. Yet they have lost ground in their #emph[spiritual frame,] or gone backward, and are entangled in many woeful decays. This is a #emph[notable] evidence of the #emph[life of sin] that we are treating of. Now the purpose of uncovering #emph[this power of sin] is that we may be careful to forestall and prevent it in its #emph[working.] And of all the effects it produces, none is more dangerous or destructive than the one we have dwelt on last: that it prevails on many #emph[professors] to an #emph[habitual decline] from their former ways and attainments, in spite of all the sweetness and excellence their souls have found in them. So, as was said, I will next consider by what #emph[ways and means,] and with what help, it usually prevails in this kind, so that we may be better instructed to watch against it.]]
